omponent block logic + processing fee). It's specifically only handling the \"quote\" operation.\n- Question: Does \"processScenario\" clearly reveal this intent?\n- Assessment: \"processScenario\" is decent but generic. The function actually computes a premium for items in a quote scenario. However, given the test structure showing multiple operations (quote, claim) with different handling coming up, \"processScenario\" as an entry point that needs to route to operation-specific logic is reasonable. As a single function handling only quote today, a more specific name would help, but since it will grow to route to quote/claim handlers, keeping \"processScenario\" as the entry dispatcher makes sense.\n\nHowever, looking at the internal logic more closely — it's all quote-specific computation. The function really computes a quote premium. For now, since there's only quote, I should evaluate the internal logic structure.\n\nLet me think about the internal variable names:\n- `items`: clear enough\n- `componentTypes`: clear — lists types that are components\n- `componentCounts`: clear — accumulator for component counts\n- `baseTotal`: clear — running total before fee\n- `PROCESSING_FEE`: clear\n- `BASE_PREMIUM`: clear\n- `ScenarioResult`: clear\n\n**Decision**: Names are clean and fit their purpose. No naming changes needed.\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\n```\nexport function processScenario(scenario: any): ScenarioResult {\n  const items = scenario.steps[0].items as any[] | undefined;\n  if (!items || items.length === 0) {\n    return { results: [{ premium: PROCESSING_FEE }] };\n  }\n\n  const componentTypes = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  let baseTotal = 0;\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (componentTypes.includes(item.type)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] || 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      baseTotal += BASE_PREMIUM[item.type];\n    }\n  }\n\n  for (const ty