e 4: Fewest Elements\n\nThe \"two loops\" pattern could be merged. After the first loop, we have the counts. We could compute block total directly from the counts without a second explicit loop. But the 2-element loop is harmless.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Implement Refactoring\n\n**Refactoring choice**: Eliminate the second loop by computing block totals directly from the collected counts after the first loop completes. This reduces a loop and associated conditionals/assignments while improving clarity by keeping all computation close together.\n\nWait — actually, the two-component loop is very small and clear. Instead, I think the bigger improvement is: remove the second loop entirely and compute block totals inline using the `componentCounts` we already built. That eliminates: the loop, a binding, a conditional, and two assignments. Less elements = lower mass. And it is equally readable.\n\nLet me implement"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3d853a337a5e4b0e9f6febe8","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_03-37-05_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_deepseek-v4-pro-no-thinking/src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"  // Process component building blocks\n  for (const type of componentTypes) {\n    const count = componentCounts[type] || 0;\n    if (count === 3) {\n      baseTotal += 60;\n    } else {\n      baseTotal += count * 25;\n    }\n  }","newText":"  // Process component building blocks: exactly 3 alike = 60 G block, else 25 G each\n  for (const type of componentTypes) {\n    const count = componentCounts[type] || 0;\n    baseTotal += count === 3 ? 60 : count * 25;\n  }"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":531,"output":2206,"cacheRead":9472,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":12209,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784950826278,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-600e